  "result": "**Root cause:** The 500s come from `parseAmount` in `src/money.js`. It couldn't handle prices with thousands separators, such as `\"2,450.50\"`, `\"1,299.00\"` and `\"1,005.99\"`. Every failing request in the log has one of those prices in the payload logged just before the error. The regex didn't match, `match()` returned `null`, and `m[1]` threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`. Only orders with a price of 1,000 or more were affected, which is why the failures looked intermittent.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- The regex now accepts properly grouped thousands separators, and the commas are stripped before the number is parsed.\n- Input that still doesn't match now throws the documented `Error('Invalid amount: <input>')` instead of a `TypeError`.\n\n**Tests (`test/money.test.js`):**\n- Parsing of the three failing prices from the log, plus `1,234,567`.\n- Malformed amounts throw `Invalid amount`. The cases are `abc`, an empty string, `1,2`, `12,34.50`, `1.234` and `-5`.\n- A `checkout` test with a `2,450.50` price, which reproduces the production failure.\n\n`npm test` passes with 7 of 7 tests.\n\nMalformed prices still end up as a 500 from `/checkout`, because `server.js` catches every error the same way. A 400 for invalid amounts might be better, but I left that alone since you didn't ask for it.",
